WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.041 --> 00:00:02.168
I've just arrived at the 
most dangerous place

00:00:02.168 --> 00:00:04.421
to be a pedestrian 
in the US.

00:00:05.422 --> 00:00:10.218
US-19 in New Port Richey, Florida
on the state's Gulf Coast.

00:00:10.927 --> 00:00:14.681
A group of urban planners looked at 
the entire US roadway network

00:00:14.681 --> 00:00:17.892
and identified 60 pedestrian 
fatality hotspots:

00:00:17.892 --> 00:00:21.396
1000 meter corridors where pedestrian deaths 
are most common.

00:00:21.730 --> 00:00:24.065
And this 1000 meter stretch 
topped their list.

00:00:24.315 --> 00:00:28.194
17 fatal crashes here in a 
16 year study window.

00:00:28.987 --> 00:00:30.655
Pedestrian fatalities in the U.S.

00:00:30.655 --> 00:00:32.991
have been creeping up 
in the past decade

00:00:32.991 --> 00:00:37.120
thanks mostly to the way road infrastructure 
favors cars above all else.

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:40.331
And nowhere is that more clear 
than right here.

00:00:47.172 --> 00:00:50.467
US-19 runs north to south, 
down Florida's Gulf Coast

00:00:50.467 --> 00:00:53.386
and bisects the small community 
of New Port Richey

00:00:53.386 --> 00:00:55.847
cutting off its downtown from 
its coastal features.

00:00:56.097 --> 00:01:00.393
Which means traveling east to west, 
or vice versa requires you to cross it.

00:01:00.518 --> 00:01:03.229
It's a type of roadway that would 
feel familiar to Americans.

00:01:03.521 --> 00:01:05.523
Car culture is visible everywhere 

00:01:05.523 --> 00:01:09.027
from the volume of drivers to 
the businesses alongside it.

00:01:10.361 --> 00:01:13.615
And along US-19, drivers 
are moving fast.

00:01:13.615 --> 00:01:16.785
The posted speed limit on this stretch 
is 45 miles per hour.

00:01:16.868 --> 00:01:19.704
But driving speeds appear to be
much faster than that.

00:01:19.996 --> 00:01:22.332
Traffic safety experts 
will tell you that speed

00:01:22.332 --> 00:01:27.295
is one of the most important factors 
in pedestrian safety.

00:01:27.629 --> 00:01:29.339
A street design report established

00:01:29.339 --> 00:01:31.674
that traveling up to 15 miles per hour

00:01:31.674 --> 00:01:33.593
drivers have a wide peripheral vision.

00:01:33.927 --> 00:01:36.721
They only need 25 feet 
to come to a full stop

00:01:36.721 --> 00:01:39.682
and the risk of fatality if 
they hit someone is 2%.

00:01:40.266 --> 00:01:43.311
Once drivers are traveling over 
40 miles per hour

00:01:43.311 --> 00:01:45.105
their peripheral view narrows.

00:01:45.105 --> 00:01:47.107
They require more room to stop

00:01:47.107 --> 00:01:49.943
and the risk of fatality 
climbs to 85%.

00:01:50.235 --> 00:01:53.113
Enforcing speed limits 
can only do so much.

00:01:53.279 --> 00:01:56.199
The built environment, 
as we call it

00:01:56.199 --> 00:01:59.869
is sending a message that this 
is for high speed travel.

00:02:00.203 --> 00:02:02.956
Robert Schneider led the 
pedestrian safety study

00:02:02.956 --> 00:02:05.458
that found that 97% of the roadways

00:02:05.458 --> 00:02:08.294
with high pedestrian fatalities 
had multiple lanes.

00:02:08.837 --> 00:02:11.881
At its widest, US-19 
spans 8 lanes

00:02:11.881 --> 00:02:14.384
which allows cars to pass 
and move faster than if

00:02:14.384 --> 00:02:16.761
they were stuck behind 
another car in a single lane.

00:02:17.262 --> 00:02:19.013
And the road is straight as an arrow.

00:02:19.013 --> 00:02:21.558
No curves that would nudge 
drivers to take it slow.

00:02:22.225 --> 00:02:26.855
The type of businesses along US-19
support a car-first fast driving environment:

00:02:26.855 --> 00:02:28.690
Big box stores and strip malls 

00:02:28.690 --> 00:02:32.443
that sit far away from the road 
with large parking lots in between.

00:02:32.443 --> 00:02:34.320
And billboards displayed up high

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:38.324
for an audience of fast drivers 
rather than at human scale.

00:02:38.908 --> 00:02:40.785
Arterial roads like US-19

00:02:40.785 --> 00:02:43.830
not quite a street, but 
not quite a highway

00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:45.790
were built to keep this
high speed traffic

00:02:45.790 --> 00:02:47.876
off of nearby residential 
neighborhoods.

00:02:48.918 --> 00:02:50.378
But this type of sprawling development

00:02:50.378 --> 00:02:52.422
grew along arterial roads

00:02:52.422 --> 00:02:55.049
creating a dangerous mix 
of car centric design

00:02:55.049 --> 00:02:59.012
with the possibility that pedestrians, 
cyclists, or public transit users

00:02:59.012 --> 00:03:01.222
would want to access these business centers.

00:03:01.431 --> 00:03:06.186
And it explains why arterial roads 
only make up 13% of US roadways

00:03:06.186 --> 00:03:10.356
but are the site of 59% 
of pedestrian deaths.

00:03:13.693 --> 00:03:17.113
Now, let's relate that to US-19's 
pedestrian design or lack thereof.

00:03:17.113 --> 00:03:19.032
or... lack thereof.

00:03:19.282 --> 00:03:21.951
Not everyone can 
or wants to drive

00:03:21.951 --> 00:03:23.995
but they still have to get to 
where they're going.

00:03:23.995 --> 00:03:27.707
I walk along US-19 about 3 or 4 times
a week, to and from work.

00:03:27.707 --> 00:03:29.167
It's a lot cheaper than taking the bus. 

00:03:29.709 --> 00:03:32.629
I was crossing the crosswalk

00:03:32.629 --> 00:03:35.089
someone was turning and just
bumped right into me. 

00:03:35.506 --> 00:03:38.134
I had a few bumps, bruises, you know,
some cracked ribs.

00:03:38.551 --> 00:03:40.303
I have a hearing disability...

00:03:40.929 --> 00:03:44.766
which causes severe vertigo
and issues with balance.

00:03:45.099 --> 00:03:47.185
So it's not wise to drive.

00:03:47.185 --> 00:03:50.146
Every single day...

00:03:52.190 --> 00:03:54.025
I am...

00:03:56.361 --> 00:03:58.154
afraid of either getting...

00:03:58.738 --> 00:04:00.823
bumped or yelled at

00:04:00.823 --> 00:04:03.451
or honked at or cut off...

00:04:03.451 --> 00:04:05.495
every day.

00:04:07.052 --> 00:04:10.262
As a pedestrian trying 
to cross the road

00:04:10.262 --> 00:04:12.047
I'd have to choose between
this crosswalk...

00:04:12.047 --> 00:04:16.353
or this one, which is nearly 
950 meters away.

00:04:16.353 --> 00:04:18.371
That's a 30 or 40 minute walk.

00:04:18.371 --> 00:04:21.715
A distance so far that it 
encourages risky jaywalking.

00:04:21.715 --> 00:04:23.758
There's a better term for that, 
it turns out.

00:04:23.758 --> 00:04:26.094
Cross at a location where 
there's no signal.

00:04:26.094 --> 00:04:28.889
In the profession, we tend to
try to say that.

00:04:28.889 --> 00:04:31.641
Jaywalk is a term that was developed

00:04:31.641 --> 00:04:35.979
by the auto interests 
in the early 1900s

00:04:35.979 --> 00:04:38.982
to essentially shame people

00:04:38.982 --> 00:04:40.734
who were crossing in the 
middle of the block

00:04:40.734 --> 00:04:45.780
which had been okay socially 
prior to the 1920s, 1930s.

00:04:46.084 --> 00:04:48.003
The sidewalks are constantly interrupted

00:04:48.003 --> 00:04:49.922
by the curb cuts into parking lots

00:04:49.922 --> 00:04:52.257
which introduce more opportunities
to interact with 

00:04:52.257 --> 00:04:53.960
a moving car without a signal.

00:04:53.960 --> 00:04:57.089
I first tried this crosswalk, 
waited for 10 minutes

00:04:57.089 --> 00:05:00.383
only to find out it didn't work,
so I tried the next crossing.

00:05:00.383 --> 00:05:02.176
Robert Schneider told me that the longer

00:05:02.176 --> 00:05:03.594
you wait at a crosswalk

00:05:03.594 --> 00:05:06.848
the more pedestrians are incentivized 
to cross before the signal.

00:05:07.334 --> 00:05:08.418
Finally, once you do cross

00:05:08.418 --> 00:05:11.005
you have to traverse the length 
of the eight lanes...

00:05:11.005 --> 00:05:15.663
which puts US-19 in the company 
of 70% of the hot spot

00:05:15.663 --> 00:05:17.469
roadways in Robert Schneider's study

00:05:17.469 --> 00:05:20.096
that force pedestrians 
to cross five or more lanes.

00:05:20.793 --> 00:05:24.209
And all of this is so much more treacherous

00:05:24.209 --> 00:05:26.659
when there is less light.

00:05:28.543 --> 00:05:31.671
There's a concept in urbanism called safety in numbers.

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:34.503
The more pedestrians there are in an environment,

00:05:34.503 --> 00:05:36.380
the safer it is to be one.

00:05:36.380 --> 00:05:39.091
There aren't many pedestrians 
along US-19

00:05:39.091 --> 00:05:40.842
which makes the high number of fatalities

00:05:40.842 --> 00:05:42.334
even more alarming.

00:05:42.334 --> 00:05:45.003
The lower the median household income
in a neighborhood

00:05:45.003 --> 00:05:48.256
the more common it is for pedestrian
fatalities to occur there.

00:05:48.836 --> 00:05:50.629
And that's because bad street design 

00:05:50.629 --> 00:05:52.631
and arterial roads like US-19

00:05:52.631 --> 00:05:55.301
are more likely to exist in those communities.

00:05:55.301 --> 00:05:58.763
Improving pedestrian design
would invite more people to walk

00:05:58.960 --> 00:06:01.796
making it safer for people 
who have no other choice.

00:06:02.584 --> 00:06:05.044
Florida transportation officials 
are spending millions

00:06:05.044 --> 00:06:07.046
to improve this stretch of US-19.

00:06:07.046 --> 00:06:10.132
Like reducing the speed limit, 
adding more crosswalks

00:06:10.132 --> 00:06:13.824
adding more lights, and delaying
green light to intersections.

00:06:14.501 --> 00:06:17.660
The mayor of New Port Richey 
told me he favors

00:06:17.660 --> 00:06:19.842
adding pedestrian and cyclist 
bridges here

00:06:19.842 --> 00:06:22.011
to get people off the road entirely.

00:06:22.011 --> 00:06:25.514
These are expensive solutions and 
unpopular among drivers.

00:06:25.514 --> 00:06:28.058
But not nearly as hard as 
the long term goal

00:06:28.058 --> 00:06:31.019
which is undoing decades 
of car centric design

00:06:31.019 --> 00:06:33.981
like removing lanes, adding 
street parking instead

00:06:33.981 --> 00:06:36.733
and developing retail and 
housing in parking lots.

00:06:36.733 --> 00:06:39.569
A built environment that will get 
more people on foot

00:06:39.569 --> 00:06:43.411
more drivers to slow down 
and will save lives.

00:06:43.411 --> 00:06:44.454
It's not a quick solution,

00:06:44.454 --> 00:06:47.999
but something we need to actually be
starting to move towards...

00:06:48.654 --> 00:06:51.448
rather than continuing to build

00:06:51.448 --> 00:06:54.910
the sprawling development that we know is dangerous.

00:07:22.859 --> 00:07:28.197
This video is an adaptation of a 
Vox.com story by reporter Marin Cogan.

00:07:28.197 --> 00:07:31.617
Local officials and journalists have credited her reporting

00:07:31.617 --> 00:07:33.911
for raising awareness about pedestrian safety

00:07:33.911 --> 00:07:35.580
along this deadly stretch of road.

00:07:36.164 --> 00:07:38.166
I highly recommend you 
check out her story 

00:07:38.166 --> 00:07:40.293
that I linked in the description below.

00:07:40.293 --> 00:07:41.752
Thanks so much for watching. 

